"""The mixer: the colors live in utils/colors.py

    python up.py             # apply, then run the guards and CI's own commands
    python up.py --check     # rehearse every edit in memory, write nothing
    python up.py --verify    # run the guards and CI's commands, change nothing

For rnv-color-mixer, derived against a fresh clone at the live head (3e04899).

RNV-DELIVERY-SCRIPT-DO-NOT-SWEEP. This script is a delivery tool, not
application source, and it names what it retires. That marker is what tells
this fleet's scanners to skip it.

RULED 2026-10-18: #63 A. Every app keeps its colors in one file, in one
place: utils/colors.py. Here they lived in utils/config.py, beside the
settings, and they leave it: every color constant, derived color, alpha,
palette, provenance map and color helper. utils/config.py keeps the
settings, paths, image folders, fonts and ThemeManager. Every import follows
the colors, and nothing reads a color from utils/config.py any more.
brand_map.json, the sync tool's chart (COLORS.md), the daily brand sync and
the docs name the new place.

WHAT LANDS.
  utils/colors.py, new: every color utils/config.py held, 77 names --
      constants, derived colors, alphas, palettes, provenance maps and the
      helpers that make a color -- with their comments, as they were.
  utils/config.py, carried whole: the settings, paths, image folders, fonts
      and ThemeManager, which import their colors from utils/colors.py.
  32 more files: every import, path and mention follows the colors;
      COLORS.md names the new place, as tools/sync_colors.py writes it.
  The locked root suite's digest, in both workflows and in the CI step here.

JUDGMENT CALLS, each reported:
  - utils/config.py imports, by name, the colors its stylesheets draw with.
    So utils.config still answers for those names; this script checks that
    no module reads a color through it.
  - The three palettes leave ThemeManager's class body for utils/colors.py.
    ThemeManager keeps DARK_THEME, LIGHT_THEME and IMAGE_THEME, the same
    three dicts, so every reader of ThemeManager.DARK_THEME reads what it
    read; a new test holds them to be the same objects, not copies.
  - RNV_Color_Mixer.py and core/palette_formats.py import their colors by
    name, because each already has a local variable called colors.
  - The locked root suite changes: its imports and reads follow the colors,
    and one docstring. Its digest is re-recorded in both workflows, and the
    CI lock step here holds the new one.
  - tests/test_brand_mirror.py lists files from git's index. It also reads
    files not yet committed (--others), so utils/colors.py is read on the
    run that adds it.
  - The README named another app's theme dicts (DARK_THEME_COLORS and the
    rest). It names this one's: DARK_THEME, LIGHT_THEME, IMAGE_THEME.
  - A note in tests/test_gold_as_text.py, the same in every app, quotes the
    palette manager's import of its colors. It names utils.colors, as that
    app's own copy does.

NO VALUE MOVES. Every value utils/config.py defined is what it was, in
utils/colors.py or where it stayed.
"""
